tem so that $x$, $y$, and $z$ appear in each equation:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3x+2y-z=1 \\\\ 0x+y-3z=0 \\\\ 0x+0y+4z=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299547 :question/worked-solution "First, let's write the system so that $x$, $y$, and $z$ appear in each equation in the correct order and all constants are on the right-hand side of the system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-5x-2y+2z=5 \\\\ 4x+3y-3z=1 \\\\ -2x+y+2z=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 3 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 3 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "First, let's write the system so that $x$, $y$, and $z$ appear in each equation in the correct order and all constants are on the right-hand side of the system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-5x-2y+2z=5 \\\\ 4x+3y-3z=1 \\\\ -2x+y+2z=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 3 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 3 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299548 :question/problem "Find the standard matrix that represents  the  linear  transformation $T$, where\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}y \\\\ x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n| $T=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |" "Find the standard matrix that represents  the  linear  transformation $T$, where\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}y \\\\ x\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n| $T=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186299548 :question/worked-solution "We require a $2\\times 2$ matrix $T$ such that\n    \n\n$$\nT\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0\\cdot x+1\\cdot y \\\\ 1\\cdot x+0\\cdot y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that\n    \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}y \\\\ x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n    Therefore, the matrix which represents the linear transformation $T$ is\n    \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "We require a $2\\times 2$ matrix $T$ such that\n    \n\n$$\nT\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0\\cdot x+1\\cdot y \\\\ 1\\cdot x+0\\cdot y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n    \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}y \\\\ x\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n    Therefore, the matrix which represents the linear transformation $T$ is\n    \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299549 :question/problem "Find the standard matrix that  represents  the  linear  transformation $T$, where\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2x-3y \\\\ -4x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n| $T=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |" "Find the standard matrix that  represents  the  linear  transformation $T$, where\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}2x-3y \\\\ -4x\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n| $T=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186299549 :question/worked-solution "We require a $2\\times 2$ matrix $T$ such that\n    \n\n$$\nT\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot x+(-3)\\cdot y \\\\ (-4)\\cdot x+0\\cdot y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that\n    \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2x-3y \\\\ -4x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n    Therefore, the matrix which represents the linear transformation $T$ is\n    \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "We require a $2\\times 2$ matrix $T$ such that\n    \n\n$$\nT\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\cdot x+(-3)\\cdot y \\\\ (-4)\\cdot x+0\\cdot y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n    \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2x-3y \\\\ -4x\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n    Therefore, the matrix which represents the linear transformation $T$ is\n    \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299550 :question/problem "Find the standard matrix that  represents  the  linear  transformation $T$, where\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}4x-y \\\\ 5x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n| $T=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |" "Find the standard matrix that  represents  the  linear  transformation $T$, where\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}4x-y \\\\ 5x\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n| $T=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186299550 :question/worked-solution "We require a $2\\times 2$ matrix $T$ such that\n    \n\n$$\nT\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot x+(-1)\\cdot y \\\\ 5\\cdot x+0\\cdot y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that\n    \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4x-y \\\\ 5x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n    Therefore, the matrix which represents the linear transformation $T$ is\n    \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "We require a $2\\times 2$ matrix $T$ such that\n    \n\n$$\nT\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4\\cdot x+(-1)\\cdot y \\\\ 5\\cdot x+0\\cdot y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n    \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4x-y \\\\ 5x\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n    Therefore, the matrix which represents the linear transformation $T$ is\n    \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299551 :question/problem "Find the standard matrix that  represents  the  linear  transformation $T$, where\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}6x \\\\ -5y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n| $T=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |" "Find the standard matrix that  represents  the  linear  transformation $T$, where\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}6x \\\\ -5y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n| $T=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186299551 :question/worked-solution "We require a $2\\times 2$ matrix $T$ such that\n    \n\n$$\nT\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}6\\cdot x+0\\cdot y \\\\ 0\\cdot x+(-5)\\cdot y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that\n    \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}6x \\\\ -5y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n    Therefore, the matrix which represents the linear transformation $T$ is\n    \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "We require a $2\\times 2$ matrix $T$ such that\n    \n\n$$\nT\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}6\\cdot x+0\\cdot y \\\\ 0\\cdot x+(-5)\\cdot y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n    \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}6x \\\\ -5y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n    Therefore, the matrix which represents the linear transformation $T$ is\n    \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299552 :question/problem "Find the standard matrix that  represents  the  linear  transformation $T$, where\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}-6x+4y \\\\ 5x-3y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n| $T=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |" "Find the standard matrix that  represents  the  linear  transformation $T$, where\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}-6x+4y \\\\ 5x-3y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n| $T=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186299552 :question/worked-solution "We require a $2\\times 2$ matrix $T$ such that\n    \n\n$$\nT\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}(-6)\\cdot x+4\\cdot y \\\\ 5\\cdot x+(-3)\\cdot y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that\n    \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-6x+4y \\\\ 5x-3y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n    Therefore, the matrix which represents the linear transformation $T$ is\n    \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "We require a $2\\times 2$ matrix $T$ such that\n    \n\n$$\nT\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}(-6)\\cdot x+4\\cdot y \\\\ 5\\cdot x+(-3)\\cdot y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n    \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-6x+4y \\\\ 5x-3y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n    Therefore, the matrix which represents the linear transformation $T$ is\n    \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299553 :question/problem "What is the linear transformation $T$ whose standard matrix is $T=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 7\\end{aligned}]?$\n\n  \n\n| $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |" "What is the linear transformation $T$ whose standard matrix is $T=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 7\\end{bmatrix}?$\n\n  \n\n| $T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186299553 :question/worked-solution "Consider a general vector $\\,[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].$ Multiplying by the matrix $T,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 7\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned