nd $det(U)=-6$, what is the value of $r?$\n\n$r=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186299602 :question/problem "If\n$S=[\\begin{aligned}3+s & 2s \\\\ -4 & 3-s\\end{aligned}]$\nand $det(S)=25,$ what is the value of $s?$\n\n$s=$\n{{field-1}}" "If\n$S=\\begin{bmatrix}3+s & 2s \\\\ -4 & 3-s\\end{bmatrix}$\nand $det(S)=25,$ what is the value of $s?$\n\n$s=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186299603 :question/problem "The vector $v$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the original vector $v?$\n\n| $v=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |" "The vector $v$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the original vector $v?$\n\n| $v=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186299603 :question/worked-solution "From the given information, we have \n\n$$\nT\\cdot v=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the inverse of $T$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{12+10}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{22}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{22}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{22}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 19\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{11} \\\\ \\frac{19}{22}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "From the given information, we have \n\n$$\nT\\cdot v=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the inverse of $T$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{12+10}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{22}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{22}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{22}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 19\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{11} \\\\ \\frac{19}{22}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299604 :question/problem "The vector $v$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the original vector $v?$\n\n| $v=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |" "The vector $v$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the original vector $v?$\n\n| $v=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186299604 :question/worked-solution "From the given information, we have \n\n$$\nT\\cdot v=[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the inverse of $T$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 1+15}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{17}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{17}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{17}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 25\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{17} \\\\ \\frac{25}{17}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "From the given information, we have \n\n$$\nT\\cdot v=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the inverse of $T$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 1+15}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{17}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{17}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{17}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 25\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{17} \\\\ \\frac{25}{17}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299605 :question/problem "The vector $v$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the original vector $v?$\n\n| $v=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |" "The vector $v$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the original vector $v?$\n\n| $v=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186299605 :question/worked-solution "From the given information, we have \n\n$$\nT\\cdot v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the inverse of $T$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{(2)(7)-(3)(5)}[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 3 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 3 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "From the given information, we have \n\n$$\nT\\cdot v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the inverse of $T$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{(2)(7)-(3)(5)}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 3 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 3 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299606 :question/problem "The vector $v$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the original vector $v?$\n\n| $v=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |" "The vector $v$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the original vector $v?$\n\n| $v=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186299606 :question/worked-solution "From the given information, we have \n\n$$\nT\\cdot v=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\nv={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the inverse of $T$, which is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{12+2}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{14}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original vector is\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{14}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{14}[\\begin{aligned}19 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{19}{14} \\\\ \\frac{3}{7}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "From the given information, we have \n\n$$\nT\\cdot v=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\nv={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the inverse of $T$, which is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{12+2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{1}{14}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original vector is\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{14}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{1}{14}\\begin{bmatrix}19 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{19}{14} \\\\ \\frac{3}{7}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299607 :question/problem "The vector $v$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the original vector $v?$\n\n| $v=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |" "The vector $v$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the original vector $v?$\n\n| $v=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186299607 :question/worked-solution "From the given information, we have \n\n$$\nT\\cdot v=[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\nv={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the inverse of $T$:\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{(5)(1)-(6)(2)}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo the original vector is\n\n$$\nv=-\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12}{7} \\\\ \\frac{4}{7}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "From the given information, we have \n\n$$\nT\\cdot v=\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\nv={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the inverse of $T$:\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{(5)(1)-(6)(2)}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -6 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}=-\\frac{1}{7}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -6 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo the original vector is\n\n$$\nv=-\\frac{1}{7}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -6 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}=-\\frac{1}{7}\\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{12}{7} \\\\ \\frac{4}{7}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299608 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that both $x$ and $y$ appear in each equation, all the variables are on the left-hand side, and all the constants are on the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-3x+4y=10 \\\\ 5x+2y=-7\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "First, notice that both $x$ and $y$ appear in each equation, all the variables are on the left-hand side, and all the constants are on the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-3x+4y=10 \\\\ 5x+2y=-7\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299609 :question/worked-solution "First, let's write the system so that both $x$ and $y$ appear in each equation. \nThe variable $x$ does not appear in the first equation, but this just means its coefficient is $0.$ So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}0x-6y=4 \\\\ 3x+5y\\,=-7\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "First, let's write the system so that both $x$ and $y$ appear in each equation. \nThe variable $x$ does not appear in the first equation, but this just means its coefficient is $0.$ So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}0x-6y=4 \\\\ 3x+5y\\,=-7\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299610 :question/worked-solution "First, let's write the system so that both $x$ and $y$ appear in each equation. The variable $y$ does not appear in the first equation, but this just means its coefficient is $0.$ Similarly, the variable $x$ does not appear in the second equation, which means that its coefficient is $0$ too. \n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-5x+0y=10 \\\\ 0x+4y=-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "First, let's write the system so that both $x$ and $y$ appear in each equation. The variable $y$ does not appear in the first equation, but this just means its coefficient is $0.$ Similarly, the variable $x$ does not appear in the second equation, which means that its coefficient is $0$ too. \n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-5x+0y=10 \\\\ 0x+4y=-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299611 :question/problem "The vector $v$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the original vector $v?$\n\n| $v=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |" "The vector $v$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the original vector $v?$\n\n| $v=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186299611 :question/worked-solution "From the given information, we have \n\n$$\nT\\cdot v=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\ v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the inverse of $T$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{(2)(-1)-(1)(1)}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-3)}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "From the given information, we have \n\n$$\nT\\cdot v=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the inverse of $T$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{(2)(-1)-(1)(1)}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-3)}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299612 :question/problem "The vector $v$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}]$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the original vector $v?$\n\n| $v=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |" "The vector $v$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the original vector $v?$\n\n| $v=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186299612 :question/worked-solution "From the given information, we have \n\n$$\nT\\cdot v=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}] \\\\ v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the inverse of $T$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{(2)(3)-(5)(1)}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}45 \\\\ -18\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "From the given information, we have \n\n$$\nT\\cdot v=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the inverse of $T$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{(2)(3)-(5)(1)}\\begi