omponent of $u.$\n\nSo, we conclude that $T$ represents a stretch of scale factor $\\frac{1}{2}$ in the $x$-direction. \n\nTherefore, the standard matrix of $T$ is\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299628 :question/problem "A linear transformation $T$ represents a stretch along the $x$-axis or $y$-axis. Given that $u=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $T(u)=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}],$ what is the standard matrix of $T?$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $T=\\,\\,$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |" "A linear transformation $T$ represents a stretch along the $x$-axis or $y$-axis. Given that $u=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $T(u)=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix},$ what is the standard matrix of $T?$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $T=\\,\\,$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186299628 :question/worked-solution "First, we are told that $T$ represents a stretch along the $x$-axis or $y$-axis.\n\nComparing the $x$- and $y$-components of $u$ and $T(u),$ we note the following:\n\n- the $x$-components of $u$ and $T(u)$ are the same, while\n\n- the $y$-component of $T(u)$ is $\\sqrt{3}$ times the $y$-component of $u.$\n\nSo, we conclude that $T$ represents a stretch of scale factor $\\sqrt{3}$ in the $y$-direction. \n\nTherefore, the standard matrix of $T$ is\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "First, we are told that $T$ represents a stretch along the $x$-axis or $y$-axis.\n\nComparing the $x$- and $y$-components of $u$ and $T(u),$ we note the following:\n\n- the $x$-components of $u$ and $T(u)$ are the same, while\n\n- the $y$-component of $T(u)$ is $\\sqrt{3}$ times the $y$-component of $u.$\n\nSo, we conclude that $T$ represents a stretch of scale factor $\\sqrt{3}$ in the $y$-direction. \n\nTherefore, the standard matrix of $T$ is\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299629 :question/worked-solution "First, let's write the system so that $x$, $y$, and $z$ appear in each equation in the correct order and all constants are on the right-hand side of the system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5x-3y+0z=3 \\\\ -2x+0y+6z=9 \\\\ 0x+7y-5z=-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "First, let's write the system so that $x$, $y$, and $z$ appear in each equation in the correct order and all constants are on the right-hand side of the system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5x-3y+0z=3 \\\\ -2x+0y+6z=9 \\\\ 0x+7y-5z=-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299630 :question/worked-solution "First, let's write the system so that $x$, $y$, and $z$ appear in each equation in the correct order and all constants are on the right-hand side of the system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+0y+4z=10 \\\\ 2x-5y+0z=-5 \\\\ -4x+3y+7z=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -5 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -5 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "First, let's write the system so that $x$, $y$, and $z$ appear in each equation in the correct order and all constants are on the right-hand side of the system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+0y+4z=10 \\\\ 2x-5y+0z=-5 \\\\ -4x+3y+7z=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -5 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -5 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299633 :question/problem "Let $a=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Find the vector projection of $a$ onto $b.$\n\n${\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,a=⟨$ \n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}\n$⟩$" "Let $a=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ 