r transformation, it must satisfy $T(kv)=kT(v).$ Noting that $[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]=2[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T([\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]) & =T(2[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =2T([\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =2[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -11\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}14 \\\\ -22\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since $T$ is a linear transformation, it must satisfy $T(kv)=kT(v).$ Noting that $\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}=2\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}) & =T(2\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =2T(\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =2\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -11\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}14 \\\\ -22\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299641 :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $\\,T\\,.$ If $\\,T(-5{v}_{1}+4{v}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}23 \\\\ -24\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,T({v}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$, then find $T({v}_{1}).$\n\n| $T({v}_{1})=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |" "Consider a linear transformation $\\,T\\,.$ If $\\,T(-5{v}_{1}+4{v}_{2})=\\begin{bmatrix}23 \\\\ -24\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,T({v}_{2})=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$, then find $T({v}_{1}).$\n\n| $T({v}_{1})=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186299641 :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(-kv+hw)=T(-kv)+T(hw)=-kT(v)+hT(w)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor any vectors $v$ and $w.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(-5{v}_{1}+4{v}_{2}) & =-5T({v}_{1})+4T({v}_{2}) \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}23 \\\\ -24\\end{aligned}] & =-5T({v}_{1})+4[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ 5T({v}_{1}) & =4[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}23 \\\\ -24\\end{aligned}] \\\\ 5T({v}_{1}) & =[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}23 \\\\ -24\\end{aligned}] \\\\ 5T({v}_{1}) & =[\\begin{aligned}-35 \\\\ 40\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-35 \\\\ 40\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{1}) & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(-kv+hw)=T(-kv)+T(hw)=-kT(v)+hT(w)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor any vectors $v$ and $w.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(-5{v}_{1}+4{v}_{2}) & =-5T({v}_{1})+4T({v}_{2}) \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}23 \\\\ -24\\end{bmatrix} & =-5T({v}_{1})+4\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ 5T({v}_{1}) & =4\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}23 \\\\ -24\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ 5T({v}_{1}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ 16\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}23 \\\\ -24\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ 5T({v}_{1}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-35 \\\\ 40\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T({v}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-35 \\\\ 40\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T({v}_{1}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299642 :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $\\,T\\,.$ If $\\,T(-6{v}_{1}+5{v}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -39\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,T({v}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$, then find $T({v}_{1}).$\n\n| $T({v}_{1})=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |" "Consider a linear transformation $\\,T\\,.$ If $\\,T(-6{v}_{1}+5{v}_{2})=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -39\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,T({v}_{2})=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$, then find $T({v}_{1}).$\n\n| $T({v}_{1})=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186299642 :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(-kv+hw)=T(-kv)+T(hw)=-kT(v)+hT(w)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor any vectors $v$ and $w.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(-6{v}_{1}+5{v}_{2}) & =-6T({v}_{1})+5T({v}_{2}) \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -39\\end{aligned}] & =-6T({v}_{1})+5[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ 6T({v}_{1}) & =5[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -39\\end{aligned}] \\\\ 6T({v}_{1}) & =[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ -15\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -39\\end{aligned}] \\\\ 6T({v}_{1}) & =[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{1}) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(-kv+hw)=T(-kv)+T(hw)=-kT(v)+hT(w)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor any vectors $v$ and $w.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(-6{v}_{1}+5{v}_{2}) & =-6T({v}_{1})+5T({v}_{2}) \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -39\\end{bmatrix} & =-6T({v}_{1})+5\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ 6T({v}_{1}) & =5\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -39\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ 6T({v}_{1}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}20 \\\\ -15\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -39\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ 6T({v}_{1}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 24\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T({v}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 24\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T({v}_{1}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299643 :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $\\,T\\,.$ If $\\,T(3{v}_{1}+{v}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,T({v}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$, then find $T({v}_{1}).$\n\n| $T({v}_{1})=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |" "Consider a linear transformation $\\,T\\,.$ If $\\,T(3{v}_{1}+{v}_{2})=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,T({v}_{2})=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$, then find $T({v}_{1}).$\n\n| $T({v}_{1})=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186299643 :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(kv+w)=T(kv)+T(w)=kT(v)+T(w)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor any vectors $v$ and $w.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(3{v}_{1}+{v}_{2}) & =3T({v}_{1})+T({v}_{2}) \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] & =3T({v}_{1})+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ 3T({v}_{1}) & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ 3T({v}_{1}) & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{1}) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(kv+w)=T(kv)+T(w)=kT(v)+T(w)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor any vectors $v$ and $w.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(3{v}_{1}+{v}_{2}) & =3T({v}_{1})+T({v}_{2}) \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix} & =3T({v}_{1})+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ 3T({v}_{1}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ 3T({v}_{1}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T({v}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T({v}_{1}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299644 :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $\\,T\\,.$ If $\\,T(3{v}_{1}+{v}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,T({v}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]$, then find $T({v}_{1}).$\n\n| $T({v}_{1})=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |" "Consider a linear transformation $\\,T\\,.$ If $\\,T(3{v}_{1}+{v}_{2})=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 13\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,T({v}_{2})=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}$, then find $T({v}_{1}).$\n\n| $T({v}_{1})=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186299644 :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(kv+w)=T(kv)+T(w)=kT(v)+T(w)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor any vectors $v$ and $w.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(3{v}_{1}+{v}_{2}) & =3T({v}_{1})+T({v}_{2}) \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}] & =3T({v}_{1})+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\ 3T({v}_{1}) & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\ 3T({v}_{1}) & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{1}) & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(kv+w)=T(kv)+T(w)=kT(v)+T(w)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor any vectors $v$ and $w.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(3{v}_{1}+{v}_{2}) & =3T({v}_{1})+T({v}_{2}) \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 13\\end{bmatrix} & =3T({v}_{1})+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ 3T({v}_{1}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 13\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ 3T({v}_{1}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T({v}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T({v}_{1}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299645 :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $\\,T\\,.$ If $\\,{v}_{1}+{v}_{2}=0\\,$ and $\\,T({v}_{1})=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\,$, find  $T(9{v}_{1}+4{v}_{2}).$\n\n| $\\,T(9{v}_{1}+4{v}_{2})=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n|  | {{field-3}} |  |  |" "Consider a linear transformation $\\,T\\,.$ If $\\,{v}_{1}+{v}_{2}=0\\,$ and $\\,T({v}_{1})=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\,$, find  $T(9{v}_{1}+4{v}_{2}).$\n\n| $\\,T(9{v}_{1}+4{v}_{2})=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n|  | {{field-3}} |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186299645 :question/worked-solution "First, since ${v}_{1}+{v}_{2}=0,$ we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{v}_{1}+4{v}_{2} & =4{v}_{1}+5{v}_{1}+4{v}_{2} \\\\  & =4({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})+5{v}_{1} \\\\  & =4(0)+5{v}_{1} \\\\  & =5{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nT(9{v}_{1}+4{v}_{2})=T(5{v}_{1}).\n$$\n\nNow, since $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that $T(kv)=kT(v).$ So \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(5{v}_{1}) & =5T({v}_{1}) \\\\  & =5[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 25\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, $T(9{v}_{1}+4{v}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 25\\end{aligned}].$" "First, since ${v}_{1}+{v}_{2}=0,$ we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{v}_{1}+4{v}_{2} & =4{v}_{1}+5{v}_{1}+4{v}_{2} \\\\  & =4({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})+5{v}_{1} \\\\  & =4(0)+5{v}_{1} \\\\  & =5{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nT(9{v}_{1}+4{v}_{2})=T(5{v}_{1}).\n$$\n\nNow, since $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that $T(kv)=kT(v).$ So \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(5{v}_{1}) & =5T({v}_{1}) \\\\  & =5\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 25\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, $T(9{v}_{1}+4{v}_{2})=\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 25\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186299646 :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $\\,T\\,.$ If $\\,{v}_{1}+{v}_{2}=0\\,$ and $\\,T({v}_{1})=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$, find  $T(7{v}_{1}+12{v}_{2}).$\n\n| $\\,T(7{v}_{1}+12{v}_{2})=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n|  | {{field-3}} |  |  |" "Consider a linear transformation $\\,T\\,.$ If $\\,{v}_{1}+{v}_{2}=0\\,$ and $\\,T({v}_{1})=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,$, find  $T(7{v}_{1}+12{v}_{2}).$\n