erse of the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & k & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\nequals $\\frac{1}{4}$ and $det(A)=-2k-3.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=${{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186299765 :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{13}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & k & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$\nequals $-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $det(A)=k-2.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" "The entry ${a}_{13}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & k & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\nequals $-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $det(A)=k-2.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=${{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186299766 :question/problem "The inverse of the matrix $U$ is found using row operations, as shown below. What are the row operations corresponding to each step?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[U\\,|\\,I] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The inverse of the matrix $U$ is found using row operations, as shown below. What are the row operations corresponding to each step?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[U\\,|\\,I] & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299766 :question/worked-solution "We write down $U$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $U$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[U\\,|\\,I] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding row operations are\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${U}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{U}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "We write down $U$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $U$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[U\\,|\\,I] & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding row operations are\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${U}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{U}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299776 :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{13}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nif its determinant is $17.$\n\n${a}_{13}^{′}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Determine the entry ${a}_{13}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nif its determinant is $17.$\n\n${a}_{13}^{′}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186299777 :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{21}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nif its determinant is $3.$\n\n${a}_{21}^{′}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Determine the entry ${a}_{21}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nif its determinant is $3.$\n\n${a}_{21}^{′}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186299778 :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{33}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nif its determinant is $-22.$\n\n${a}_{33}^{′}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Determine the entry ${a}_{33}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nif its determinant is $-22.$\n\n${a}_{33}^{′}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186299781 :question/problem "Calculate $F+G,$ where $F=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 7\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$G=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 10 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n| $F+G=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |" "Calculate $F+G,$ where $F=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 7\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$G=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 10 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n| $F+G=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186299781 :question/worked-solution "We add the corresponding entries:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}F+G & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 7\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 10 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3+(-9) & -4+10 \\\\ -2+5 & 7+(-1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We add the corresponding entries:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}F+G & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 7\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 10 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3+(-9) & -4+10 \\\\ -2+5 & 7+(-1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299782 :question/problem "Calculate $P+Q,$ where\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 9 & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2 & 11\\end{aligned}]$ and $Q=[\\begin{aligned}10 & -5 & -8 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -6\\end{aligned}].$\n\n| $P+Q=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-4}} | {{field-5}} | {{field-6}} |  |  |" "Calculate $P+Q,$ where\n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 9 & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2 & 11\\end{bmatrix}$ and $Q=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & -5 & -8 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -6\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n| $P+Q=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-4}} | {{field-5}} | {{field-6}} |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186299782 :question/worked-solution "We add the corresponding entries:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P+Q & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 9 & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2 & 11\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}10 & -5 & -8 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4+10 & 9+(-5) & 3+(-8) \\\\ 7+(-1) & -2+4 & 11+(-6)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 4 & -5 \\\\ 6 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We add the corresponding entries:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P+Q & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 9 & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2 & 11\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}10 & -5 & -8 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4+10 & 9+(-5) & 3+(-8) \\\\ 7+(-1) & -2+4 & 11+(-6)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 4 & -5 \\\\ 6 & 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299783 :question/problem "Calculate $C-D$, where\n$C=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 12 & -1 \\\\ 6 & -7 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ and $D=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 5 & 2 \\\\ -8 & -1 & 10\\end{aligned}].$\n\n| $C-D=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-4}} | {{field-5}} | {{field-6}} |  |  |" "Calculate $C-D$, where\n$C=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 12 & -1 \\\\ 6 & -7 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $D=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 5 & 2 \\\\ -8 & -1 & 10\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n| $C-D=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-4}} | {{field-5}} | {{field-6}} |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186299783 :question/worked-solution "We subtract the corresponding entries:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-D & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 12 & -1 \\\\ 6 & -7 & 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 5 & 2 \\\\ -8 & -1 & 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4-(-9) & 12-5 & -1-2 \\\\ 6-(-8) & -7-(-1) & 3-10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 14 & -6 & -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We subtract the corresponding entries:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-D & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 12 & -1 \\\\ 6 & -7 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 5 & 2 \\\\ -8 & -1 & 10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4-(-9) & 12-5 & -1-2 \\\\ 6-(-8) & -7-(-1) & 3-10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 14 & -6 & -7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299784 :question/problem "Calculate $G-H$, where\n$G=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 7 \\\\ -6 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -8\\end{aligned}]$ and $H=[\\begin{aligned}10 & -5 \\\\ -6 & -9 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n| $G-H=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |\n|  | {{field-5}} | {{field-6}} |  |  |" "Calculate $G-H$, where\n$G=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 7 \\\\ -6 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -8\\end{bmatrix}$ and $H=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & -5 \\\\ -6 & -9 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n| $G-H=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |\n|  | {{field-5}} | {{field-6}} |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186299784 :question/worked-solution "We subtract the corresponding entries:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}G-H & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 7 \\\\ -6 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -8\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}10 & -5 \\\\ -6 & -9 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7-10 & 7-(-5) \\\\ -6-(-6) & 2-(-9) \\\\ 5-3 & -8-(-1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 11 \\\\ 2 & -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We subtract the corresponding entries:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}G-H & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 7 \\\\ -6 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -8\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}10 & -5 \\\\ -6 & -9 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7-10 & 7-(-5) \\\\ -6-(-6) & 2-(-9) \\\\ 5-3 & -8-(-1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 11 \\\\ 2 & -7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299785 :question/problem "Calculate $P-Q$, where\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 & 9 \\\\ 7 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 10 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]$ and $Q=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -8 & 6 \\\\ -4 & 3 & -7 \\\\ 12 & -5 & 9\\end{aligned}].$\n\n| $P-Q=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | $3$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $11$ | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |\n|  | {{field-5}} | $5$ | {{field-6}} |  |  |" "Calculate $P-Q$, where\n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 4 & 9 \\\\ 7 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 10 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $Q=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -8 & 6 \\\\ -4 & 3 & -7 \\\\ 12 & -5 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n| $P-Q=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | $3$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $11$ | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |\n|  | {{field-5}} | $5$ | {{field-6}} |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186299785 :question/worked-solution "We subtract the corresponding entries:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P-Q & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 & 9 \\\\ 7 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 10 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & -8 & 6 \\\\ -4 & 3 & -7 \\\\ 12 & -5 & 9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6-1 & 4-(-8) & 9-6 \\\\ 7-(-4) & -2-3 & 5-(-7) \\\\ 10-12 & 0-(-5) & -3-9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & \\,\\,12 & 3 \\\\ \\,\\,\\,11 & -5 & 12 \\\\ -2 & 5 & -12\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We subtract the corresponding entries:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P-Q & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 4 & 9 \\\\ 7 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 10 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -8 & 6 \\\\ -4 & 3 & -7 \\\\ 12 & -5 & 9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6-1 & 4-(-8) & 9-6 \\\\ 7-(-4) & -2-3 & 5-(-7) \\\\ 10-12 & 0-(-5) & -3-9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & \\,\\,12 & 3 \\\\ \\,\\,\\,11 & -5 & 12 \\\\ -2 & 5 & -12\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299786 :question/problem "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nW=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Y=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4} & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Z=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nW=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,Y=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{4} & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,Z=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186299786 :question/worked-solution "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrix $W$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{3}{4}{R}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{3}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =W\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $Y$ is not an elementary matrix because it can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\n- The matrix $Z$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $W$ and $Z$ are elementary matrices." "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrix $W$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{3}{4}{R}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{3}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =W\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $Y$ is not an elementary matrix because it can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\n- The matrix $Z$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $W$ and $Z$ are elementary matrices."] [:db/cas 17592186299801 :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ k & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nequals $-\\frac{1}{2}.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" "The entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ k & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nequals $-\\frac{1}{2}.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=${{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186299802 :question/problem "Evaluate $AC+BC$, where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -4\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & \\sqrt{6} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n|  |  | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $AC+BC=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | $0$ | $0$ |  |\n|  |  | $3$ | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |  |" "Evaluate $AC+BC$, where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -4\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & \\sqrt{6} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n|  |  | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $AC+BC=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | $0$ | $0$ |  |\n|  |  | $3$ | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186299802 :question/worked-solution "We can apply the distributive law\n\n$$\nAC+BC=(A+B)C.\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $A+B$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+B & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -4\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC+BC & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & \\sqrt{6} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 3\\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We can apply the distributive law\n\n$$\nAC+BC=(A+B)C.\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $A+B$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+B & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -4\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC+BC & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & \\sqrt{6} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 3\\sqrt{6}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299803 :question/problem "Calculate $AB+AC$, where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & \\sqrt{3}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n|  |  | $0$ | $0$ | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $AB+AC=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} | $\\sqrt{3}$ | $2$ |  |\n|  |  | $0$ | {{field-3}} | $0$ |  |" "Calculate $AB+AC$, where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & \\sqrt{3}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n|  |  | $0$ | $0$ | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $AB+AC=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} | $\\sqrt{3}$ | $2$ |  |\n|  |  | $0$ | {{field-3}} | $0$ |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186299803 :question/worked-solution "We can apply the distributive law\n\n$$\nAB+AC=A(B+C).\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $B+C$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B+C & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 & \\sqrt{3}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & \\sqrt{3} & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB+AC & =A(B+C) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & \\sqrt{3} & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & \\sqrt{3} & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We can apply the distributive law\n\n$$\nAB+AC=A(B+C).\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $B+C$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B+C & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & \\sqrt{3}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & \\sqrt{3} & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB+AC & =A(B+C) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & \\sqrt{3} & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & \\sqrt{3} & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299807 :question/problem "Calculate $CB-CA$, where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -5\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n|  |  | $0$ | {{field-1}} | $0$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $CB-CA=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} | $0$ | {{field-3}} |  |\n|  |  | $0$ | {{field-4}} | $0$ |  |" "Calculate $CB-CA$, where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -5\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n|  |  | $0$ | {{field-1}} | $0$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $CB-CA=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} | $0$ | {{field-3}} |  |\n|  |  | $0$ | {{field-4}} | $0$ |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186299807 :question/worked-solution "We apply the distributive law\n\n$$\nCB-CA=C(B-A).\n$$\n\nSo, compute $B-A$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-A & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -5\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-3{I}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CB-CA & =C(B-A) \\\\  & =C(-3{I}_{3}) \\\\  & =-3C \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We apply the distributive law\n\n$$\nCB-CA=C(B-A).\n$$\n\nSo, compute $B-A$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -5\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-3{I}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CB-CA & =C(B-A) \\\\  & =C(-3{I}_{3}) \\\\  & =-3C \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299808 :question/problem "Calculate $AC-BC$, where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -4\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n|  |  | $4$ | $0$ | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $AC-BC=\\,\\,$ |  | $0$ | $-4$ | {{field-2}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} | $4$ |  |" "Calculate $AC-BC$, where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -4\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n|  |  | $4$ | $0$ | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $AC-BC=\\,\\,$ |  | $0$ | $-4$ | {{field-2}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} | $4$ |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186299808 :question/worked-solution "We apply the distributive law on the right:\n\n$$\nAC-BC=(A-B)C.\n$$\n\nSo, compute $A-B$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-B & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4{I}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC-BC & =(A-B)C \\\\  & =(4{I}_{3})C \\\\  & =4C \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We apply the distributive law on the right:\n\n$$\nAC-BC=(A-B)C.\n$$\n\nSo, compute $A-B$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-B & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =4{I}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC-BC & =(A-B)C \\\\  & =(4{I}_{3})C \\\\  & =4C \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299809 :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{23}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & k & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nequals $-1.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" "The entry ${a}_{23}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & k & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nequals $-1.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=${{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186299810 :question/problem "Calculate $ABC$, where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $ABC=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} |  |" "Calculate $ABC$, where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $ABC=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186299810 :question/worked-solution "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $3\\times 1$ matrix, $B$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix, and $C$ is a $2\\times 1$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $3\\times 2$ matrix, which will require finding $6$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $1\\times 1$ matrix, which will require finding only $1$ entry.\n\nSo, we find the product $BC$ first: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-13\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $A$ by $BC:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =A(BC) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-13\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-65 \\\\ 13 \\\\ -39\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $3\\times 1$ matrix, $B$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix, and $C$ is a $2\\times 1$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $3\\times 2$ matrix, which will require finding $6$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $1\\times 1$ matrix, which will require finding only $1$ entry.\n\nSo, we find the product $BC$ first: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-13\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $A$ by $BC:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =A(BC) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-13\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-65 \\\\ 13 \\\\ -39\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299811 :question/problem "Calculate $ABC$, where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $ABC=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} |  |" "Calculate $ABC$, where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $ABC=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186299811 :question/worked-solution "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $3\\times 1$ matrix, $B$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix, and $C$ is a $2\\times 1$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $3\\times 2$ matrix, which will require finding $6$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $1\\times 1$ matrix, which will require finding only $1$ entry.\n\nSo, we find the product $BC$ first: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-20-6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-26\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $A$ by $BC:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =A(BC) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-26\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}78 \\\\ -52 \\\\ -182\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $3\\times 1$ matrix, $B$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix, and $C$ is a $2\\times 1$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $3\\times 2$ matrix, which will require finding $6$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $1\\times 1$ matrix, which will require finding only $1$ entry.\n\nSo, we find the product $BC$ first: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-20-6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-26\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $A$ by $BC:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =A(BC) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-26\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}78 \\\\ -52 \\\\ -182\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299812 :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{32}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & -2 \\\\ k & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nequals $-2.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" "The entry ${a}_{32}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & -2 \\\\ k & -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nequals $-2.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=${{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186299814 :question/problem "Calculate $ABC$, where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}6 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" rowspan=\"2\">$ABC=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" "Calculate $ABC$, where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" rowspan=\"2\">$ABC=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [:db/cas 17592186299814 :question/worked-solution "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix, $B$ is a $2\\times 1$ matrix, and $C$ is a $1\\times 3$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $1\\times 1$ matrix, which will require finding only $1$ entry, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $2\\times 3$ matrix, which will require finding $6$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $AB$ first: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6\\cdot (-1)+(-2)\\cdot 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6-6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-12\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $(AB)$ by $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =(AB)\\,C \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-12\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-24 & 48 & -60\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix, $B$ is a $2\\times 1$ matrix, and $C$ is a $1\\times 3$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $1\\times 1$ matrix, which will require finding only $1$ entry, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $2\\times 3$ matrix, which will require finding $6$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $AB$ first: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6\\cdot (-1)+(-2)\\cdot 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6-6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-12\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $(AB)$ by $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =(AB)\\,C \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-12\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-24 & 48 & -60\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299815 :question/problem "Calculate $ABC$, where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 6\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n| $ABC=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |" "Calculate $ABC$, where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 6\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n| $ABC=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |"] [:db/cas 17592186299815 :question/worked-solution "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix, $B$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix, and $C$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $1\\times 2$ matrix, which will require finding $2$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $2\\times 2$ matrix, which will require finding $4$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $AB$ first: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & 34\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $AB$ by $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =(AB)C \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & 34\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-58 & 136\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix, $B$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix, and $C$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $1\\times 2$ matrix, which will require finding $2$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $2\\times 2$ matrix, which will require finding $4$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $AB$ first: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 34\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $AB$ by $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =(AB)C \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 34\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-58 & 136\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299816 :question/problem "Calculate $3A$, where\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}].$\n\n| $3A=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |" "Calculate $3A$, where\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n| $3A=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186299816 :question/worked-solution "We multiply each entry of $A$ by $3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3A & =3[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot 2 & 3\\cdot 1 \\\\ 3\\cdot 4 & 3\\cdot 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ 12 & 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We multiply each entry of $A$ by $3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3A & =3\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\cdot 2 & 3\\cdot 1 \\\\ 3\\cdot 4 & 3\\cdot 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 3 \\\\ 12 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299819 :question/problem "Calculate $-7A$, where\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ -6 & 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n| $-7A=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |" "Calculate $-7A$, where\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ -6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n| $-7A=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186299819 :question/worked-solution "We multiply each entry of $A$ by $3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7A & =-7[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ -6 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(-7)\\cdot 5 & (-7)\\cdot 4 \\\\ (-7)\\cdot (-6) & (-7)\\cdot 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-35 & -28 \\\\ 42 & -14\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We multiply each entry of $A$ by $3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7A & =-7\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ -6 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(-7)\\cdot 5 & (-7)\\cdot 4 \\\\ (-7)\\cdot (-6) & (-7)\\cdot 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-35 & -28 \\\\ 42 & -14\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299820 :question/problem "Calculate $2C$, where\n$C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}].$\n\n| $2C=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |" "Calculate $2C$, where\n$C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n| $2C=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186299820 :question/worked-solution "We multiply each entry of $C$ by $2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2C & =2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot 1 & 2\\cdot 2 \\\\ 2\\cdot (-2) & 2\\cdot 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We multiply each entry of $C$ by $2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2C & =2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\cdot 1 & 2\\cdot 2 \\\\ 2\\cdot (-2) & 2\\cdot 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299831 :question/problem "Given the matrix\n$B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],$ find ${B}^{-1}$ by performing row operations." "Given the matrix\n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},$ find ${B}^{-1}$ by performing row operations."] [:db/cas 17592186299831 :question/worked-solution "We consider the augmented matrix $[B\\,|\\,I]$ where $I$ is the $3\\times 3$ identity matrix. Applying the indicated row operations we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[B\\,|\\,I] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 5 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:=-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 5 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  \\\\  & =[I\\,|\\,{B}^{-1}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse matrix is \n\n$$\n{B}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "We consider the augmented matrix $[B\\,|\\,I]$ where $I$ is the $3\\times 3$ identity matrix. Applying the indicated row operations we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[B\\,|\\,I] & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & {R}_{2}:=-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  \\\\  & =[I\\,|\\,{B}^{-1}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse matrix is \n\n$$\n{B}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299833 :question/worked-solution "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -7 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -7 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{aligned}].$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 6-(-7)\\cdot (-3)}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{15}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{15}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -7 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -7 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 6-(-7)\\cdot (-3)}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{15}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{15}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299834 :question/problem "Given the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],$ find ${A}^{-1}$ by performing row operations." "Given the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},$ find ${A}^{-1}$ by performing row operations."] [:db/cas 17592186299834 :question/worked-solution "We consider the augmented matrix $[A\\,|\\,I]$ where $I$ is the $3\\times 3$ identity matrix. Applying the indicated row operations we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:=(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  \\\\  & =[I\\,|\\,{A}^{-1}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse matrix is \n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "We consider the augmented matrix $[A\\,|\\,I]$ where $I$ is the $3\\times 3$ identity matrix. Applying the indicated row operations we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I] & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & {R}_{1}:=(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  \\\\  & =[I\\,|\\,{A}^{-1}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse matrix is \n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299837 :question/worked-solution "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{aligned}].$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 6-(-3)\\cdot (-3)}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 6-(-3)\\cdot (-3)}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299839 :question/problem "Let\n$C=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$D=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -1 & 5\\end{aligned}].$\nThen, the dimensions of $CD$ are{{field-1}}$\\times $\n{{field-2}}." "Let\n$C=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$D=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.$\nThen, the dimensions of $CD$ are{{field-1}}$\\times $\n{{field-2}}."] [:db/cas 17592186299840 :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the elementary matrices corresponding to the two given elementary row operations.\n\n- The first elementary row operation is ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={E}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second elementary row operation is ${R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2}$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={E}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo apply the first elementary row operation to the matrix $A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{1}$ on the left and get ${E}_{1}A:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A\n$$\n\nThen, to apply the second elementary row operation to ${E}_{1}A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{2}$ on the left and get ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A:$ \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A\n$$" "First, let's find the elementary matrices corresponding to the two given elementary row operations.\n\n- The first elementary row operation is ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={E}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second elementary row operation is ${R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2}$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={E}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo apply the first elementary row operation to the matrix $A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{1}$ on the left and get ${E}_{1}A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A\n$$\n\nThen, to apply the second elementary row operation to ${E}_{1}A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{2}$ on the left and get ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299841 :question/problem "Let\n$E=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 5 \\\\ 7 & -6\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$F=[\\begin{aligned}9 & -2 & 8 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 6 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].$\nThen, the dimensions of $FE$ are{{field-1}}$\\times $\n{{field-2}}." "Let\n$E=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 5 \\\\ 7 & -6\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$F=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -2 & 8 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 6 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.$\nThen, the dimensions of $FE$ are{{field-1}}$\\times $\n{{field-2}}."] [:db/cas 17592186299842 :question/problem "Let  $E=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ Find the row operation that, when applied to a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ gives the result of the product $EA.$" "Let  $E=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find the row operation that, when applied to a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ gives the result of the product $EA.$"] [:db/cas 17592186299842 :question/worked-solution "The matrix $E$ is obtained by multiplying the first row of the identity matrix  by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $EA$ is equivalent to applying the row operation ${R}_{1}:=3{R}_{1}$ to $A.$" "The matrix $E$ is obtained by multiplying the first row of the identity matrix  by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $EA$ is equivalent to applying the row operation ${R}_{1}:=3{R}_{1}$ to $A.$"] [:db/cas 17592186299843 :question/problem "Let\n$E=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$F=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$ Then, the dimensions of $EF$ are{{field-1}}$\\times $\n{{field-2}}." "Let\n$E=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$F=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Then, the dimensions of $EF$ are{{field-1}}$\\times $\n{{field-2}}."] [:db/cas 17592186299844 :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we get the vectors\n\n$$\n\\text{u}=⟨-3,2⟩,\\,\\text{v}=⟨1,3⟩.\n$$\n\nFirst, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-9-2 \\\\  & =-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|-11|=11\n$$" "From the diagram, we get the vectors\n\n$$\n\\text{u}=⟨-3,2⟩,\\,\\text{v}=⟨1,3⟩.\n$$\n\nFirst, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-9-2 \\\\  & =-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|-11|=11\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299845 :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we get the vectors\n\n$$\n\\text{u}=⟨4,-1⟩,\\,\\text{v}=⟨-2,-3⟩.\n$$\n\nFirst, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-3)-(-2)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-12-2 \\\\  & =-14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|-14|=14\n$$" "From the diagram, we get the vectors\n\n$$\n\\text{u}=⟨4,-1⟩,\\,\\text{v}=⟨-2,-3⟩.\n$$\n\nFirst, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-3)-(-2)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-12-2 \\\\  & =-14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|-14|=14\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299846 :question/problem "Determine the area $A$ of the parallelogram spanned by $\\text{u}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ and $\\text{v}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$\n\n$\\,\\,A=$ {{field-1}}" "Determine the area $A$ of the parallelogram spanned by $\\text{u}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\text{v}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n$\\,\\,A=$ {{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186299846 :question/worked-solution "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 4-5\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-8-15 \\\\  & =-23\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|\\,-23\\,|=23\n$$" "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 4-5\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-8-15 \\\\  & =-23\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|\\,-23\\,|=23\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299847 :question/problem "Determine the area $A$ of the parallelogram spanned by $\\text{u}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ and $\\text{v}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].$\n\n$\\,\\,A=$ {{field-1}}" "Determine the area $A$ of the parallelogram spanned by $\\text{u}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\text{v}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n$\\,\\,A=$ {{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186299847 :question/worked-solution "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot 5-(-2)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =15-2 \\\\  & =13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|\\,13\\,|=13\n$$" "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 5-(-2)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =15-2 \\\\  & =13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|\\,13\\,|=13\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299848 :question/problem "Determine the area $A$ of the parallelogram spanned by $\\text{u}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]$ and $\\text{v}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n$\\,\\,A=$ {{field-1}}" "Determine the area $A$ of the parallelogram spanned by $\\text{u}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\text{v}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n$\\,\\,A=$ {{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186299848 :question/worked-solution "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 5 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 5 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-2)-5\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =2-35 \\\\  & =-33\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|\\,-33\\,|=33\n$$" "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 5 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 5 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-2)-5\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =2-35 \\\\  & =-33\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|\\,-33\\,|=33\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299849 :question/worked-solution "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1-(-3)\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =2-(-21) \\\\  & =23\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|\\,23\\,|=23\n$$" "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1-(-3)\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =2-(-21) \\\\  & =23\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|\\,23\\,|=23\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299850 :question/worked-solution "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}-4 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-4)\\cdot 2-6\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-8-(-6) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|\\,-2\\,|=2\n$$" "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-4)\\cdot 2-6\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-8-(-6) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|\\,-2\\,|=2\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299851 :question/worked-solution "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =5\\cdot (-4)-(-2)\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =-20-6 \\\\  & =-26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|\\,-26\\,|=26\n$$" "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =5\\cdot (-4)-(-2)\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =-20-6 \\\\  & =-26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|\\,-26\\,|=26\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299853 :question/problem "If $kB=[\\begin{aligned}33 & 36 \\\\ -12 & -24\\end{aligned}]$,\nwhere $B=[\\begin{aligned}11 & 12 \\\\ -4 & -8\\end{aligned}]$\nand $k$ is a scalar, calculate the value of $k.$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" "If $kB=\\begin{bmatrix}33 & 36 \\\\ -12 & -24\\end{bmatrix}$,\nwhere $B=\\begin{bmatrix}11 & 12 \\\\ -4 & -8\\end{bmatrix}$\nand $k$ is a scalar, calculate the value of $k.$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186299853 :question/worked-solution "To express $kB,$ we multiply each entry of $B$ by $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kB & =k[\\begin{aligned}11 & 12 \\\\ 4 & -8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}11k & 12k \\\\ 4k & -8k\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the above result equal to the matrix given for $kB$ in the problem statement:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}11k & 12k \\\\ -4k & -8k\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}33 & 36 \\\\ -12 & -24\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by equating any two corresponding (nonzero) elements. Let's choose the bottom-left elements, $-4k$ and $-12.$ Equating these elements, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4k & =-12 \\\\ k & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We would still get the same result if we chose any other two corresponding (nonzero) elements." "To express $kB,$ we multiply each entry of $B$ by $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kB & =k\\begin{bmatrix}11 & 12 \\\\ 4 & -8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}11k & 12k \\\\ 4k & -8k\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the above result equal to the matrix given for $kB$ in the problem statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}11k & 12k \\\\ -4k & -8k\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}33 & 36 \\\\ -12 & -24\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by equating any two corresponding (nonzero) elements. Let's choose the bottom-left elements, $-4k$ and $-12.$ Equating these elements, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4k & =-12 \\\\ k & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We would still get the same result if we chose any other two corresponding (nonzero) elements."] [:db/cas 17592186299854 :question/worked-solution "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2} & a \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2} & a \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot 6-a\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =9+5a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, setting the expression for the determinant equal to $0$ and solving for $a,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9+5a & =0 \\\\ 5a & =-9 \\\\ a & =-\\frac{9}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{2} & a \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{3}{2} & a \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot 6-a\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =9+5a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, setting the expression for the determinant equal to $0$ and solving for $a,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9+5a & =0 \\\\ 5a & =-9 \\\\ a & =-\\frac{9}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299855 :question/problem "If $kW=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 20 & -15 \\\\ 10 & -10 & 25\\end{aligned}]$,\nwhere $W=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]$\nand $k$ is a scalar, calculate the value of $k.$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" "If $kW=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 20 & -15 \\\\ 10 & -10 & 25\\end{bmatrix}$,\nwhere $W=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$\nand $k$ is a scalar, calculate the value of $k.$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186299855 :question/worked-solution "To express $kW,$ we multiply each entry of $W$ by $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kW & =k[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}k & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4k & -3k \\\\ 2k & -2k & 5k\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the above result equal to the matrix given for $kW$ in the problem statement:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}k & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4k & -3k \\\\ 2k & -2k & 5k\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 20 & -15 \\\\ 10 & -10 & 25\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by equating any two corresponding (nonzero) elements. Let's choose the  leftmost elements of the third row, $2k$ and $10.$ Equating these elements, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2k & =10 \\\\ k & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We would still get the same result if we chose any other two corresponding (nonzero) elements." "To express $kW,$ we multiply each entry of $W$ by $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kW & =k\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}k & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4k & -3k \\\\ 2k & -2k & 5k\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the above result equal to the matrix given for $kW$ in the problem statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}k & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4k & -3k \\\\ 2k & -2k & 5k\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 20 & -15 \\\\ 10 & -10 & 25\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by equating any two corresponding (nonzero) elements. Let's choose the  leftmost elements of the third row, $2k$ and $10.$ Equating these elements, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2k & =10 \\\\ k & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We would still get the same result if we chose any other two corresponding (nonzero) elements."] [:db/cas 17592186299856 :question/worked-solution "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{3} & a \\\\ 7 & -9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{3} & a \\\\ 7 & -9\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-\\frac{4}{3})\\cdot (-9)-a\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =12-7a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, setting the expression for the determinant equal to $0$ and solving for $a,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12-7a & =0 \\\\ 7a & =12 \\\\ a & =\\frac{12}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{4}{3} & a \\\\ 7 & -9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{4}{3} & a \\\\ 7 & -9\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{4}{3})\\cdot (-9)-a\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =12-7a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, setting the expression for the determinant equal to $0$ and solving for $a,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12-7a & =0 \\\\ 7a & =12 \\\\ a & =\\frac{12}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299857 :question/worked-solution "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{2} & a \\\\ -\\sqrt{5} & 10\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{2} & a \\\\ -\\sqrt{5} & 10\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 10-a\\cdot (-\\sqrt{5}) \\\\  & =30\\sqrt{2}+a\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, setting the expression for the determinant equal to $0$ and solving for $a,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}30\\sqrt{2}+a\\sqrt{5} & =0 \\\\ a\\sqrt{5} & =-30\\sqrt{2} \\\\ a & =-\\frac{30\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ a & =-\\frac{30\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{5}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ a & =-\\frac{30\\sqrt{10}}{5} \\\\ a & =-6\\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}3\\sqrt{2} & a \\\\ -\\sqrt{5} & 10\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3\\sqrt{2} & a \\\\ -\\sqrt{5} & 10\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 10-a\\cdot (-\\sqrt{5}) \\\\  & =30\\sqrt{2}+a\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, setting the expression for the determinant equal to $0$ and solving for $a,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}30\\sqrt{2}+a\\sqrt{5} & =0 \\\\ a\\sqrt{5} & =-30\\sqrt{2} \\\\ a & =-\\frac{30\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ a & =-\\frac{30\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{5}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ a & =-\\frac{30\\sqrt{10}}{5} \\\\ a & =-6\\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299858 :question/problem "Given that the area spanned between the vectors $\\text{u}=[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ and $\\text{v}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]$ equals zero, what is the value of $a?$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}" "Given that the area spanned between the vectors $\\text{u}=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\text{v}=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}$ equals zero, what is the value of $a?$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186299858 :question/worked-solution "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{2} & a \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{2} & a \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(2\\sqrt{2})\\cdot 6-a\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{2}-3a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, setting the expression for the determinant equal to $0$ and solving for $a,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12\\sqrt{2}-3a & =0 \\\\ -3a & =-12\\sqrt{2} \\\\ a & =4\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{2} & a \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2\\sqrt{2} & a \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(2\\sqrt{2})\\cdot 6-a\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{2}-3a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, setting the expression for the determinant equal to $0$ and solving for $a,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12\\sqrt{2}-3a & =0 \\\\ -3a & =-12\\sqrt{2} \\\\ a & =4\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299859 :question/problem "Given that the area spanned between the vectors $\\text{u}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{7} \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$ and $\\text{v}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]$ equals zero, what is the value of $a?$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}" "Given that the area spanned between the vectors $\\text{u}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{7} \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\text{v}=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}$ equals zero, what is the value of $a?$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186299859 :question/worked-solution "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{7} & a \\\\ 5 & -10\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{7} & a \\\\ 5 & -10\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-\\sqrt{7})(-10)-a\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =10\\sqrt{7}-5a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, setting the expression for the determinant equal to $0$ and solving for $a,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10\\sqrt{7}-5a & =0 \\\\ 5a & =10\\sqrt{7} \\\\ a & =2\\sqrt{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{7} & a \\\\ 5 & -10\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\sqrt{7} & a \\\\ 5 & -10\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-\\sqrt{7})(-10)-a\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =10\\sqrt{7}-5a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, setting the expression for the determinant equal to $0$ and solving for $a,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10\\sqrt{7}-5a & =0 \\\\ 5a & =10\\sqrt{7} \\\\ a & =2\\sqrt{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299860 :question/problem "If $kB=[\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 & 2\\end{aligned}]$,\nwhere $B=[\\begin{aligned}24 & -12 & 6\\end{aligned}]$\nand $k$ is a scalar, calculate the value of $k.$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" "If $kB=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$,\nwhere $B=\\begin{bmatrix}24 & -12 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$\nand $k$ is a scalar, calculate the value of $k.$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186299860 :question/worked-solution "To express $kB,$ we multiply each entry of $B$ by $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kB & =k[\\begin{aligned}24 & -12 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}24k & -12k & 6k\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the above result equal to the matrix given for $kB$ in the problem statement:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}24k & -12k & 6k\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by equating any two corresponding (nonzero) elements. Let's choose the rightmost elements, $6k$ and $2.$ Equating these elements, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6k & =2 \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We would still get the same result if we chose any other two corresponding (nonzero) elements." "To express $kB,$ we multiply each entry of $B$ by $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kB & =k\\begin{bmatrix}24 & -12 & 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}24k & -12k & 6k\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the above result equal to the matrix given for $kB$ in the problem statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}24k & -12k & 6k\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by equating any two corresponding (nonzero) elements. Let's choose the rightmost elements, $6k$ and $2.$ Equating these elements, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6k & =2 \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We would still get the same result if we chose any other two corresponding (nonzero) elements."] [:db/cas 17592186299869 :question/problem "Calculate $4F-\\frac{1}{2}G$, where\n$F=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$G=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 4 & -6\\end{aligned}].$\n\n| $4F-\\frac{1}{2}G=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |" "Calculate $4F-\\frac{1}{2}G$, where\n$F=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$G=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 4 & -6\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n| $4F-\\frac{1}{2}G=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |"] [:db/cas 17592186299869 :question/worked-solution "We multiply each entry of $F$ by $4$ and each entry of $G$ by $\\frac{1}{2},$ and then subtract the resulting matrices, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4F-\\frac{1}{2}G & =4[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 4 & -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot 4 & 4\\cdot (-1) & 4\\cdot 0 & 4\\cdot 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2 & \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4 & \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-6)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & -4 & 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}16-1 & -4-0 & 0-2 & 8-(-3)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}15 & -4 & -2 & 11\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We multiply each entry of $F$ by $4$ and each entry of $G$ by $\\frac{1}{2},$ and then subtract the resulting matrices, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4F-\\frac{1}{2}G & =4\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 4 & -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4\\cdot 4 & 4\\cdot (-1) & 4\\cdot 0 & 4\\cdot 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2 & \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4 & \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-6)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}16 & -4 & 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}16-1 & -4-0 & 0-2 & 8-(-3)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}15 & -4 & -2 & 11\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299873 :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{2}{3}M+2N$, where\n$M=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 & 15 \\\\ -12 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$N=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & \\frac{1}{2} & -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n| $\\frac{2}{3}M+2N=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | $-2$ | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $-12$ | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |" "Calculate $\\frac{2}{3}M+2N$, where\n$M=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 & 15 \\\\ -12 & 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$N=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & \\frac{1}{2} & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n| $\\frac{2}{3}M+2N=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | $-2$ | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $-12$ | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186299873 :question/worked-solution "We multiply each entry of $M$ by $\\frac{2}{3}$ and each entry of $N$ by $2,$ and then add the resulting matrices, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}M+2N & =\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 & 15 \\\\ -12 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & \\frac{1}{2} & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 6 & \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 0 & \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 15 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (-12) & \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 6 & \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot 3 & 2\\cdot (-1) & 2\\cdot 0 \\\\ 2\\cdot (-2) & 2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} & 2\\cdot (-1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 10 \\\\ -8 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}6 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4+6 & 0-2 & 10+0 \\\\ -8-4 & 4+1 & 0-2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & -2 & 10 \\\\ -12 & 5 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We multiply each entry of $M$ by $\\frac{2}{3}$ and each entry of $N$ by $2,$ and then add the resulting matrices, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}M+2N & =\\frac{2}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 & 15 \\\\ -12 & 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+2\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & \\frac{1}{2} & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 6 & \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 0 & \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 15 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (-12) & \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 6 & \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2\\cdot 3 & 2\\cdot (-1) & 2\\cdot 0 \\\\ 2\\cdot (-2) & 2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} & 2\\cdot (-1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 10 \\\\ -8 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4+6 & 0-2 & 10+0 \\\\ -8-4 & 4+1 & 0-2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}10 & -2 & 10 \\\\ -12 & 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299897 :question/worked-solution "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 1-4\\cdot (-3)}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{14}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{14}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 1-4\\cdot (-3)}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{14}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{14}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299898 :question/worked-solution "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot 3-0\\cdot (-6)}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot 3-0\\cdot (-6)}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299899 :question/worked-solution "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{0\\cdot (-1)-2\\cdot 5}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -5 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -5 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{align